      "textEn": "229\nare capable of serving. In this way, in the state of divine bliss, it happened that the senses\nwere eagerly awaiting for the service of God. By this happening, Narad Muni got lost in seeing himself and God together.\nRupam Bhagavato yattamanahkantam shuchapaham.\nApashyant sahasottasthe vaikalyavaharmana iv. 18>. Meaning of the word\nForm - form; Bhagavata - of the Lord; Yat - which is; Tat_that; Manah - of the mind; Kantam_that; Shuch -\nApaham - removing all distinctions; Apashyant - without seeing; Sahasa - suddenly; Uttashe - stood up;\nVaivalyat - becoming distressed; Durmanah - losing the desired; Iva mano_that\n\nThe divine form of God, as it is, fulfills the desires of the mind and immediately removes all\nmental anguish. Therefore, upon losing that form, I became restless,\nand stood up, as it often happens when one's desired object is lost. Note: Narada Muni realised that the Lord is formless.\nBut their form\nis completely different from all forms of our physical experience. Throughout our lives, in this material world,\nwe keep seeing different forms, but none of them satisfy the mind, nor can any of them\nremove the unrest of our minds. This is the specialty of the divine form of God,\nand whoever has seen that form even once is not satisfied with any other object. No form of the physical world can satisfy the seer. 'God is formless or\nformless' This means that he has no physical form and he is not like any\nphysical personality.\n\nAs spiritual beings, because of being related to that divine form of God, we\nall keep searching for the same form of God through every birth and are not satisfied with any other\nform of physical satisfaction. Narad Muni had a glimpse of this, but not seeing it again,\nhe became agitated and suddenly stood up to search for it. We, for generations, whose"
