      "textEn": "230\nThey search, he had found Narad Muni, therefore his disappearance was certainly shocking to them. I, seeing that, again, bowing my mind.\nEven while looking, I did not see, being unsatisfied, restless. 19. Shabdartham\nDidshuh- willing to see; Tat-- that; Aham-- I; Bhuyah- again; Pranidhaya-- concentrating the mind; Manah- mind;\nHadi- in the heart; Vikshamaanah- looking to see; Api- while; Na- never; Apashyam- having seen;\nAvitrup- without being satisfied; Iva- like; Aturah- agitated..\n\nI wanted to see that divine form of God again, but being focused in my heart,\nDespite all my efforts to see that form, I could not see them again. In this way, when I became dissatisfied, I became extremely angry. Meaning: There is no mechanical method to see the form of God ©. It completely depends on\nGod's unconditional grace.\nWe cannot ask God to\nappear before our sight, just as we cannot ask the sun to\nrise according to our will. The sun rises voluntarily, in the same way,\nGod also appears by his unselfish grace. A person should wait for the appropriate opportunity\nand continue to perform his appointed duties for the devotional service of God. Narad Muni\nthought that he would be able to see God again in the same mechanical method, by which he had succeeded in the first attempt, but even after trying very hard, his second attempt could not be successful. God is free from all kinds of bondage. They can only be bound by the bondage of exclusive devotion. They are neither visible to our senses nor perceptible to our senses. When they are pleased with the devotional service's\ndevout effort, and when they want to, then they appear willingly. And the one who is engaged in the forest, the one who is not visible."
