      "textEn": "232\n\nMeaning: In the Bhagavad Gita, God is described as extremely pure, supreme, and the ultimate truth. In their personality, there is not even a trace of materialism; therefore, the person in whom\nthere is even a trace of material attachment cannot reach them. The beginning of devotional service\nhappens when a person is freed from at least two physical qualities—the quality of passion and the quality of ignorance—\nand becomes free. Being free from lust and greed is its characteristic. The meaning is that man\nshould be freed from the desires of sensual gratification and the thirst for sensual pleasure. Of nature,\nthe balanced quality is the quality of goodness, and to be free from all material impurities, one has to rise above the quality of goodness as well. Going to a secluded forest and searching for the vision (experience) of God\ncomes under the quality of goodness. A man can go to the forest for spiritual accomplishment, but it does not mean that he will have a direct vision of God there.\nA human being should be free from all material\nattachments and should be situated on the plane of spirituality, only then can he\nreceive the presence of God. Therefore, the best method is that man should go and live there,\nwhere the divine form of God is worshiped. A temple of God is a divine place,\nwhile a forest is an excellent dwelling place from a physical point of view. Even a newly initiated devotee is not told to go to the forest to search for God, but to worship the idol of God.\nIt is said. Devotion begins with the worship of God, and it is superior to going to the forest. Narad Muni does not need to go to the forest in his current life,\nbecause he is free from all material desires and he can transform every place with his presence\ninto Vaikuntha. They keep wandering from one world to another to make humans, gods, Kinnaras, Gandharvas, sages, ascetics, and others\ndevotees of God.\nHe, through his\nactivities, has engaged many devotees like TEE Maharaj, Dhruv Maharaj, in the divine service of God. Therefore, on the footprints of great devotees like Narada and Prahmad, who are pure devotees of God"
