      "textEn": "235\n\nThe intellect engaged in my devotion is never separated. Even until the time of creation,\nalong with the time of destruction, your memory will remain by my grace.\n\nMeaning: Devotion to God never goes in vain. Because God is the creator,\ntherefore, the intellect applied in his service or any action taken towards him is permanent. It is said in the Bhagavad Gita that such divine service done to God continues until birth after birth.\nIt keeps accumulating, and when the devotee becomes fully mature, then all his service\nBy integrating it, he becomes worthy of going into the presence of God. Such service to God\nis never destroyed, but keeps growing until it is fully mature.\nHaving spoken thus, I bow to that great one.\nThe earth, the water, the sky, and the mountains,\nI, too, am greatly honored by Him.\nHe, having heard my plea, has shown mercy. 25. Meaning of the word:\nEtavat-in this way; Uktya-to say; Uparama-stopped; Tat-that; Mahat-great; Bhutam-amazing; Nabha-the sky;\nLikhham-expressed by word; Alidrumu-not visible to the eyes, invisible; Ichham-me; Cha-also;\nTasmai-to them; Mahatam-great; Mahiyaase-adorned with greatness; Sheershna-from the head; Avanamam-bowing; Vidadhe-did;\nAnukampitah-by showing kindness to them..\n\nThen, the one who is expressed by words and invisible to the eyes, that extremely astonishing supreme being, stopped speaking. I bowed my head, feeling grateful, and paid my respects. Meaning: it does not matter if God is only heard or not seen. The Lord created the four Vedas with his breath.\nThey are seen and\nexperienced only through the divine utterance of the Vedas. Similarly, the Bhagavad Gita is the word-expression of God, and there is no difference between them and\ntheir form. The conclusion is that God can be seen and heard continuously through the utterance of divine sound."
