      "textEn": "236\n\nNamanyanantasya hataatrapaha pathantu\nGuhyani bhadrani krutani cha smarantu. The one who is satisfied with the sight of the tourist,\nwaits for time, is delighted, and is free from envy. 26). Meaning of the word:\nNamani--holy names, fame, etc.; Anantasya--of the infinite; Hata-trapah--free from all worldly formalities; Pathan--through chanting, through recitation; Guhyani--mysterious; Bhadrani--beneficial; Krutani--work; Cha--and; Smaran--by constantly remembering; Gam--on earth; Paritran--wandering everywhere; Tushtamanah--being fully satisfied; Gatam--having gone;\nSprhah--being completely free from all material desires; Kalam--time, of time; Pratikshan--waiting;\nVimadah--without pride; Vimatsarah--without envy; Gam--on; Gam--on; Gam--on;\nSparshah--without touch; Gam--on; Gam--on; Gam--on; Gam--on; Gam--on; Gam--on; Gam--on; Gam--on; Gam--on; Gam--.\n\nIn this way, neglecting all the formalities of the physical world, I\nbegan to chant the holy name and glory of HER God. The divine\nkirtan and remembrance of the Lord's divine plays is beneficial.\nIn this way, I, completely,\nbeing satisfied, humble, and free from envy, began to wander all over the earth. Meaning: Narad Muni has described in brief the life of a devout devotee of God through his personal example. Such a devotee, upon receiving initiation from God or from an authentic representative of God,\ndevoutly chants the glory of God and wanders throughout the world, so that other people may also hear the glory of God. Such devotees\nhave no desire for material gain. Their only wish, their desire is that they return to the abode of God. At the right time, upon abandoning the physical body, they get this opportunity. Because going back to the abode of God is the ultimate goal of their life, therefore they neither envy anyone, nor do they have pride in being worthy to go to the abode of God.\nTheir\nonly business is to chant and remember the holy name, fame, and pastimes of God, and\nto propagate the message for the welfare of others, devoid of the desire for material gain according to one's personal ability."
